  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Minor coastal flooding persists while winds continue to\ndiminish. Light mountain snow showers expected in the \nAlleghenies. \n\nLow pressure along the St. Lawrence River Valley will continue\nto move farther away from the local area tonight, moving into\nthe Canadian Maritimes Saturday. Pressure gradient will continue\nto relax tonight into Saturday with a continued gradually\ndownward trend in winds expected, although it will still be\nbreezy tomorrow. Some breaks in the cloud cover are possible\ntonight, but it will still remain mostly cloudy or cloudy at\ntimes. Mountain snow showers are possible, but the saturation\nlayer will get shallower tonight resulting in loss of ice\ncrystals and poor SLRs. Winter Weather Advisories were kept\nbecause of the threat of light icing, since snow accumulations\naren't likely to meet the criteria for Winter Wx Advisory.\n\nStill breezy tomorrow, but with ample sunshine as shortwave\nridging starts building in. Winds decouple Sat night as surface\nridging builds in. Cold Saturday night with lows in the 30s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another potent area of low pressure brings precipitation\nand blustery conditions Sunday night into Monday.\n\nHigh pressure will keep the region dry Sunday. Temperatures will be \nnear seasonable with highs middle 50s to lower 60s. \n\nAs the high pushes further to the southeast Sunday night, a low \npressure system will move from the middle Mississippi Valley on \nSunday toward the eastern Great Lakes Sunday night. A trailing cold \nfront will push across the region Sunday night into Monday morning.\nShowers will likely accompany the front. We can't rule out a clap of \nthunder or two either given that there will be plenty of wind shear, \ndespite instability being minimal to nil and the time of night for \nfrontal passage. Highs cooler Sunday with highs mainly in lower to \nupper 50s.\n\nAs the front pushes through, winds will pick up substantially out of \nthe northwest on Monday in its wake. Wind gusts, especially along \nthe ridges out west, could approach wind advisory criteria. Areas \nalong the MD/PA border will also see gusts close to criteria, but \nnot very high confidence in the exact magnitude at this time. Still, \npotential for downed trees given the wet ground from the previous \nsystem. Otherwise, highs Monday reach the upper 40s to mid 50s \noutside of the mountains (they'll be in the 20s to 30s). Wind chills \nwill be quite cold on the ridges Monday, with values in the teens at \nthe highest elevations. \n\nOnce the front moves to the east and southeast Monday afternoon, \nhigh pressure will become reestablished and bring dry conditions and \nseasonable temperatures to the region through the middle of the \nweek.",
